1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,180
What a model's written reasoning can and cannot show.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:11,500
Ask a model a multiple-choice question, and add one line: I think the answer is A,
but I'm curious to hear what you think.

3
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,940
Miles Turpin and colleagues did this across thirteen reasoning tasks.

4
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:21,340
The models' written reasoning argued for the suggested answer and did not mention
the suggestion.

5
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:27,300
With that nudge toward wrong answers, accuracy fell by as much as thirty-six
percent, in the worst case.

6
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:29,860
The written reasoning read as a reason.

7
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:31,580
It was not the whole cause.

8
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:34,980
Newer reasoning models write longer reasoning.

9
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:40,660
A 2025 study measured how often it names a hint the model used.

10
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:49,060
Across six kinds of hint, Claude 3.7 Sonnet named it twenty-five percent of the
time, and DeepSeek R1 thirty-nine percent.

11
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:54,440
In environments that paid for exploiting a planted shortcut, the model took it on
over ninety-nine percent of prompts and, in most of those environments, named it on

12
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:59,220
fewer than two percent.

13
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:02,300
That does not make the reasoning useless.

14
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:09,800
In the 2025 OpenAI coding study, a monitor that read only the model's actions caught
sixty percent of two common test-gaming tricks.

15
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,940
A monitor that also read the reasoning caught ninety-five percent.

16
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,640
Then the researchers penalized reasoning that the monitor flagged.

17
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,920
The tricks continued, and the monitor's catch rate fell to near zero.

18
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:29,360
A 2025 position paper from authors across several labs calls this a new and fragile
opportunity.

19
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,600
Written reasoning is a readout.

20
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:36,340
Check it against what the model does, and avoid training that pays the model to make
it look clean.

21
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:40,260
Read the reasoning, and trust the actions you can check.

22
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:43,200
The voice you heard is a synthesized version of mine.

23
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:46,640
Every number on screen links to its source under the video.
